## 2. PayPal integration: APIs and transaction flow

Create a new Spring Boot module or microservice named `paypal-funding-service`. Keeping it separate means you can implement and test PayPal without disturbing your existing Ledger.

### API endpoints to implement

| Endpoint                                            | Purpose                                                         |
| --------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `POST /api/funding/paypal/orders`                   | Create a PayPal sandbox order for a server-calculated amount    |
| `POST /api/funding/paypal/orders/{orderId}/capture` | Capture an approved order through PayPal                        |
| `GET /api/funding/{fundingId}`                      | Return the funding attempt and ledger-credit status             |
| `POST /api/webhooks/paypal`                         | Optional but valuable: receive and verify PayPal webhook events |

These are your application's endpoints, not PayPal's API URLs.

Your service calls PayPal's `POST /v2/checkout/orders` endpoint to create an order and `POST /v2/checkout/orders/{id}/capture` to capture it. Use the sandbox base URL `https://api-m.sandbox.paypal.com`.&#x20;

