## 7. Three decisions to make before coding

1. PayPal funding semantics: use sandbox payments to credit a clearly labelled internal demo wallet, not a claim of real bank-backed stored value.
2. AI implementation: prefer a Java-compatible model to preserve your existing stack; use Python only if the chosen model or development speed justifies a separate service.
3. Scope: implement one working merchant simulator rather than trying to finish QR, NFC, and Bluetooth simultaneously.

One final architecture consideration: keep your rule engine responsible for deterministic security checks. AI should add risk intelligence, not replace signature verification, token expiry enforcement, idempotency, or ledger integrity.

If you implement these pieces cleanly, the project will tell a coherent story: PayPal funds the demo wallet, your existing system enables offline spending, and AI helps evaluate the risk when transactions synchronize. That is a much stronger submission than simply attaching a PayPal checkout button to the existing project.